{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788372388999,"sessionID":"ses_f9cb500feffeJAzwf2KlQEhiz0","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_9f9b3536c8ab493a9b96660f","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /tmp/opencode && python3 << 'EOF'\nimport csv\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef col(path, name, enc='utf-8'):\n    with open(path, encoding=enc) as f:\n        return [r.get(name) for r in csv.DictReader(f)]\n\nprint(\"Cup rounds:\", Counter(col('/workspace/data/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv','round')))\nprint()\nprint(\"Lib stages:\", Counter(col('/workspace/data/kaggle/Libertadores_Matches.csv','stage')))\nprint()\nprint(\"BR-FD tournaments:\", Counter(col('/workspace/data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv','tournament')))\nprint()\nprint(\"Hist Vencedor:\", Counter(col('/workspace/data/kaggle/novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv','Vencedor')))\nEOF"},"output":"Cup rounds: Counter({'2': 376, '1': 334, '3': 258, '4': 140, '5': 123, '6': 62, '7': 30, '8': 14})\n\nLib stages: Counter({'group stage': 960, 'round of 16': 160, 'quarterfinals': 80, 'semifinals': 40, 'final': 15})\n\nBR-FD tournaments: Counter({'Serie A': 3689, 'Serie B': 3677, 'Serie C': 1807, 'Copa do Brasil': 1123})\n\nHist Vencedor: Counter({'Mandante': 3488, 'Empate': 1795, 'Visitante': 1603})\n","metadata":{"output":"Cup rounds: Counter({'2': 376, '1': 334, '3': 258, '4': 140, '5': 123, '6': 62, '7': 30, '8': 14})\n\nLib stages: Counter({'group stage': 960, 'round of 16': 160, 'quarterfinals': 80, 'semifinals': 40, 'final': 15})\n\nBR-FD tournaments: Counter({'Serie A': 3689, 'Serie B': 3677, 'Serie C': 1807, 'Copa do Brasil': 1123})\n\nHist Vencedor: Counter({'Mandante': 3488, 'Empate': 1795, 'Visitante': 1603})\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd /tmp/opencode && python3 << 'EOF'\nimport csv\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef col(path, name, enc='utf-8'):\n    with open(path, encoding=enc) as f:\n        return [r.get(name) for r in csv.DictReader(f)]\n\nprint(\"Cup rounds:\", Counter(col('/workspace/data/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv','round')))\nprint()\nprint(\"Lib stages:\", Counter(col('/workspace/data/kaggle/Libertadores_Matches.csv','stage')))\nprint()\nprint(\"BR-FD tournaments:\", Counter(col('/workspace/data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv','tournament')))\nprint()\nprint(\"Hist Vencedor:\", Counter(col('/workspace/data/kaggle/novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv','Vencedor')))\nEOF","time":{"start":1788372388976,"end":1788372388984}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The normalization works well. Key observations:\n\n1. My key function (strip parentheticals, strip state suffix, strip accents, lowercase, alphanumeric only) groups names well.\n2. Problem cases:\n   - **\"Atletico\" collisions**: 'Atletico - PR', 'Atletico-GO', 'Atletico-MG', 'Atlético - AC', 'Atlético - BA' — these are DIFFERENT clubs (Atlético Paranaense vs Atlético Mineiro vs Atlético Goianiense vs Atlético Alagoano etc.) that all normalize to \"atletico\" when the state suffix is stripped! This is bad. The state suffix is the discriminator for \"Atlético-PR\" vs \"Atlético-MG\" vs \"Atlético-GO\".\n   \n   So the state suffix should NOT always be stripped — it should be part of the identity when the base name is ambiguous. \"Flamengo-RJ\" vs \"Flamengo-PI\" are different clubs. \"Botafogo-RJ\" vs \"Botafogo-PB\" are different. \"Santos-SP\" vs \"Santos-AP\" different!\n   \n   Better approach: use **base name + state** as the canonical key: \"flamengo-rj\" vs \"flamengo-pi\". E.g. \"Atlético-PR\" -> \"atleticopr\" or \"atletico-pr\". Then \"Athletico Paranaense\" would also map to \"atletico-pr\" via alias.\n\n   Actually, the cleaner canonical key: `name_state` where available. But not all datasets have state. E.g. BR-Football-Dataset \"Sao Paulo\" has no state. Libertadores \"Boca Juniors\" no country.\n\n   Approach: canonical key = base + optional state. For \"Sao Paulo\" (no state in BR-Football-Dataset), key = \"saopaulo\". For \"São Paulo - SP\", base=\"saopaulo\", state=\"SP\" — since there's only one \"Sao Paulo\" club in the data (Santos-AP is \"Santos\" different base), we can resolve \"saopaulo\" + \"sp\" -> \"saopaulo-sp\"... but then \"saopaulo\" wouldn't match \"saopaulo-sp\".\n   \n   Resolution strategy: \n   1. Extract base and state from each raw name.\n   2. Build mapping from raw names to a canonical team identity.\n   3. For a base name, if all occurrences share one club or the state disambiguates, we can resolve.\n   \n   Simpler: build aliases per club. A \"club\" = (base_key, state) where state may be None. Two names match if base keys equal AND (states equal OR one of the states is None and the base is unambiguous).\n   \n   Let me think about what matters for the task: answering questions about the famous clubs — Flamengo, Corinthians, Palmeiras, São Paulo, Santos, Grêmio, etc. For those, base name is unambiguous enough. Atlético-MG vs Atlético-PR: users asking \"Atlético\" is ambiguous anyway; with state suffix we can distinguish.\n\n   Design: \n   - Parse each raw name -> (base_key, state_or_None, country_or_None).\n   - Canonical team = base_key + (\"-\" + state.lower() if state else \"\").\n   - When querying by a user-supplied name (e.g., \"Atlético-MG\" or \"Palmeiras\" or \"São Paulo FC\"), parse similarly: \"Palmeiras\" -> base \"palmeiras\", no state. Match any team whose base_key == \"palmeiras\" (there's only palmeiras-sp). If user gives \"Atlético-MG\" -> base \"atletico\", state \"mg\" -> matches atletico-mg only.\n   - For \"Atletico\" without state -> ambiguous: matches multiple (atletico-pr, atletico-go, atletico-mg, atletico-ac...). The tool could then return the ambiguity or pick... For a query \"Atlético Paranaense\" -> base after removing filler words... hmm, \"athleticoparanaense\" != \"atletico\". Need alias: \"atletico paranaense\" == \"atletico-pr\", \"atletico mineiro\" == \"atletico-mg\".\n\n   Let me build a curated alias table for Brazilian clubs (the famous ones at least):\n   - atleticopr: Atlético-PR, Athletico-PR, Athletico Paranaense, Atlético Paranaense\n   - atleticomg: Atlético-MG, Atlético Mineiro, Clube Atlético Mineiro\n   - atleticogo: Atlético-GO, Atlético Goianiense\n   - saopaulo: São Paulo, Sao Paulo, SPFC, Tricolor Paulista\n   - corinthians: Corinthians, SC Corinthians Paulista, Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\n   - etc.\n\n   Let me check the FIFA data club names for Brazilian clubs to see what forms appear there. Let me query.\n\n3. Foreign clubs in Libertadores (\"Nacional (URU)\", \"Barcelona-EQU\", \"Guaraní (PAR)\") — country suffix in parens or dashes. My regex strips parentheticals first, so \"Nacional (URU)\" -> \"Nacional\" — collides with \"Nacional-AM\" (Brazilian). And \"Guaraní (PAR)\" -> \"guarani\" collides with \"Guarani-SP\". For famous Brazilian club queries this matters little, but head-to-head between foreign clubs could mismatch. To be safer: treat parenthetical country and dash-country as \"state\" too. \"Nacional (URU)\" -> (base \"nacional\", \"URU\"). \"Barcelona-EQU\" -> (\"barcelona\", \"EQU\"). Then nacional-am vs nacional-uru are distinct. Good.\n\nBut wait: dash-suffix extraction on \"Atletico Mineiro\"? \"Mineiro\" is not a 2-3 letter code, so no strip. Good. What about names ending in a 2-3 letter uppercase word that is part of the name, like \"PSTC\", \"URT\", \"CRB\", \"CSA\", \"ABC\", \"ASA\", \"CRAC\"? \"Pstc - PR\" -> base \"pstc\" state \"pr\". Plain \"PSTC\" -> base \"pstc\" state None. Match: base equal, state None matches any. OK. But \"CRB\" alone: if I apply the suffix regex `[\\s-]+[A-Z]{2,3}$` — \"CRB\" has no dash/space before, so untouched. \"A.b.c. - RN\" -> parenthetical none; dash suffix strips \" - RN\" -> \"A.b.c.\" -> key \"abc\". Good.\n\nEdge: \"Botafogo-PB\" vs \"Botafogo-RJ\" — distinct clubs, good with state. \"Botafogo\" plain in BR-Football-Dataset? Let me check which states appear. Also \"Flamengo\" plain probably refers to Flamengo-RJ (famous) but there's also Flamengo-PI. For matching user queries, \"Flamengo\" base matches both flamengo-rj and flamengo-pi; for stats tools I might default to the most prominent (the one with most matches in top divisions — RJ). Hmm, but simply matching \"any team with base 'flamengo'\" would mix two clubs' data. \n\nPragmatic approach: when the user queries a name without a state and multiple clubs share the base, prefer the club with the most matches in the dataset overall (famous clubs play way more matches), and mention alternatives. Or simply include all matches from all matching clubs — for Flamengo-PI vs Flamengo-RJ the PI one has very few matches (probably from Copa do Brasil first rounds). Including them barely skews stats but pollutes results. I'll do \"most prominent\" resolution with a note.\n\nActually, simpler and more predictable: \n- `resolve_team(name)` returns the best-matching canonical club: exact base+state match if state given; else if only one club with that base, that one; else the one with most total matches (most prominent); also handle alias table for full names like \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\".\n\nAlias table also needed for FIFA club names. Let me check FIFA club names now.\n\n4. Dates: parse \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\", \"29/03/2003\", \"2023-09-24\". Standard parsers handle these.\n\n5. For \"finals\" of Copa do Brasil: round column values — let me check unique rounds in the cup file and stages in Libertadores.\n\nLet me also check:\n- unique tournaments in BR-Football-Dataset\n- unique rounds in Brazilian_Cup_Matches\n- unique stages in Libertadores\n- FIFA club names for Brazilian clubs\n- Date ranges per file/season.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0634d048a001eSL7JX2QgPjl4l","sessionID":"ses_f9cb500feffeJAzwf2KlQEhiz0","messageID":"msg_0634c85b6001mZiAVkGvGEldTC"}}
