{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788372430377,"sessionID":"ses_f9cb500feffeJAzwf2KlQEhiz0","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_7f7de610ea6340c58a3b1704","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /tmp/opencode && python3 << 'EOF'\nimport csv\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef seasons(path, col, enc='utf-8'):\n    with open(path, encoding=enc) as f:\n        return Counter(r.get(col,'') for r in csv.DictReader(f))\n\nprint(\"Brasileirao_Matches:\", dict(sorted(seasons('/workspace/data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv','season').items())))\nprint(\"Cup:\", dict(sorted(seasons('/workspace/data/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv','season').items())))\nprint(\"Lib:\", dict(sorted(seasons('/workspace/data/kaggle/Libertadores_Matches.csv','season').items())))\nprint(\"BR-FD:\", dict(sorted(seasons('/workspace/data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv','tournament').items())))\nprint(\"Hist:\", dict(sorted(seasons('/workspace/data/kaggle/novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv','Ano').items())))\nEOF"},"output":"Brasileirao_Matches: {'2012': 380, '2013': 380, '2014': 380, '2015': 380, '2016': 380, '2017': 380, '2018': 380, '2019': 380, '2020': 380, '2021': 380, '2022': 380}\nCup: {'2012': 112, '2013': 158, '2014': 159, '2015': 158, '2016': 162, '2017': 120, '2018': 120, '2019': 120, '2020': 120, '2021': 108}\nLib: {'2013': 126, '2014': 126, '2015': 126, '2016': 126, '2017': 126, '2018': 126, '2019': 125, '2020': 125, '2021': 124, '2022': 124, 'NA': 1}\nBR-FD: {'Copa do Brasil': 1123, 'Serie A': 3689, 'Serie B': 3677, 'Serie C': 1807}\nHist: {'2003': 552, '2004': 552, '2005': 462, '2006': 380, '2007': 380, '2008': 380, '2009': 380, '2010': 380, '2011': 380, '2012': 380, '2013': 380, '2014': 380, '2015': 380, '2016': 380, '2017': 380, '2018': 380, '2019': 380}\n","metadata":{"output":"Brasileirao_Matches: {'2012': 380, '2013': 380, '2014': 380, '2015': 380, '2016': 380, '2017': 380, '2018': 380, '2019': 380, '2020': 380, '2021': 380, '2022': 380}\nCup: {'2012': 112, '2013': 158, '2014': 159, '2015': 158, '2016': 162, '2017': 120, '2018': 120, '2019': 120, '2020': 120, '2021': 108}\nLib: {'2013': 126, '2014': 126, '2015': 126, '2016': 126, '2017': 126, '2018': 126, '2019': 125, '2020': 125, '2021': 124, '2022': 124, 'NA': 1}\nBR-FD: {'Copa do Brasil': 1123, 'Serie A': 3689, 'Serie B': 3677, 'Serie C': 1807}\nHist: {'2003': 552, '2004': 552, '2005': 462, '2006': 380, '2007': 380, '2008': 380, '2009': 380, '2010': 380, '2011': 380, '2012': 380, '2013': 380, '2014': 380, '2015': 380, '2016': 380, '2017': 380, '2018': 380, '2019': 380}\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd /tmp/opencode && python3 << 'EOF'\nimport csv\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef seasons(path, col, enc='utf-8'):\n    with open(path, encoding=enc) as f:\n        return Counter(r.get(col,'') for r in csv.DictReader(f))\n\nprint(\"Brasileirao_Matches:\", dict(sorted(seasons('/workspace/data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv','season').items())))\nprint(\"Cup:\", dict(sorted(seasons('/workspace/data/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv','season').items())))\nprint(\"Lib:\", dict(sorted(seasons('/workspace/data/kaggle/Libertadores_Matches.csv','season').items())))\nprint(\"BR-FD:\", dict(sorted(seasons('/workspace/data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv','tournament').items())))\nprint(\"Hist:\", dict(sorted(seasons('/workspace/data/kaggle/novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv','Ano').items())))\nEOF","time":{"start":1788372430364,"end":1788372430373}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The FIFA data has only 11 Brazilian clubs (FIFA 19 licensed ones): Santos, Grêmio, Cruzeiro, Fluminense, Botafogo, Internacional, Bahia, Sport Club do Recife, Ceará Sporting Club, Chapecoense, Vitória, Paraná, Atlético Mineiro. That's 13 actually. So \"highest rated players at Flamengo\" can't be answered from this data — fine, the spec's examples were generic. Players queries should handle whatever's there.\n\nNote: FIFA data clubs use full names like \"Sport Club do Recife\", \"Ceará Sporting Club\", \"Atlético Mineiro\". So the alias map needs to link these to match data team names:\n- \"Sport Club do Recife\" -> sport-pe\n- \"Ceará Sporting Club\" -> ceara-ce\n- \"Atlético Mineiro\" -> atletico-mg\n- \"Chapecoense\" -> chapecoense-sc\n- \"Vitória\" -> vitoria-ba\n- \"Paraná\" -> parana-pr\n- \"Grêmio\" -> gremio-rs\n- \"Santos\" -> santos-sp\n- \"Cruzeiro\" -> cruzeiro-mg\n- \"Fluminense\" -> fluminense-rj\n- \"Botafogo\" -> botafogo-rj\n- \"Internacional\" -> internacional-rs\n- \"Bahia\" -> bahia-ba\n\nNow the design is clear. Canonical team identity: (base_key, state). I'll build:\n\n1. `TeamResolver` with:\n   - `parse(name)` -> (base_key, state_or_none)\n   - alias map from full names (\"sport club do recife\" -> base \"sport\", state PE; \"atlético mineiro\" -> \"atletico\"+\"MG\")\n   - resolution of query names to canonical teams\n\n2. Data loader: `MatchRecord` dataclass with fields: date, season, competition, round_or_stage, home_team (canonical display name), away_team, home_goals, away_goals, source. Plus raw home/away for reference.\n\nCompetitions (normalized):\n- Brasileirao_Matches.csv -> \"Brasileirão Serie A\" (2012-2023?)\n- novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv -> \"Brasileirão Serie A\" (historical 2003-2019) — overlap years 2012-2019 with the other file! Hmm, that would double count. Wait: Brasileirao_Matches has 4180 matches. 2023 season would be 380 matches. 4180 = 11 seasons × 380 = 2013-2023? Let me check seasons. The other has 6886 matches from 2003-2019 (17 seasons). Overlap 2012(?) -2019. Both are \"Brasileirão Serie A\". If I merge them under the same competition, standings for 2019 would be computed from duplicated data. Need to check: do both files cover the same seasons? Let me check the seasons in each.\n\nActually wait — let me check whether the two Brasileirão files overlap. If Brasileirao_Matches covers 2012-2023 and the historical covers 2003-2019, then overlapping seasons are duplicated. I should check whether the matches are duplicates. Best: dedupe matches by (date, home, away, score) when merging, or keep them as separate sources but prefer one. Let me check.\n\n3. For \"competition\" names in BR-Football-Dataset: 'Serie A', 'Serie B', 'Serie C', 'Copa do Brasil'. These have richer stats (corners, shots, attacks) — 2018-2023 probably.\n\nLet me check seasons per file and overlap between the two Brasileirão files. Also date parsing for BR-Football-Dataset 'date' column format (2023-09-24) and 'time'.\n\nThen design tools:\n\nMCP tools (names + params):\n1. `find_matches(team=None, opponent=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, competition=None, season=None, stage=None, limit=20)` — search matches.\n2. `head_to_head(team_a, team_b)` — H2H record.\n3. `team_stats(team, season=None, competition=None, venue=None)` — W/D/L, GF/GA, win rate.\n4. `search_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_overall=None, limit=20, sort_by_overall=True)` — player queries.\n5. `top_players(nationality=\"Brazil\", limit=10)` — could be done via search_players but keep separate? I'll fold into search_players.\n6. `standings(competition, season)` — computed table (for Brasileirão Serie A & historical).\n7. `relegated_teams(competition, season)` — bottom 4.\n8. `champion(competition, season)` — top of standings.\n9. `competition_info(competition)` — seasons available, matches count, teams.\n10. `statistics(competition=None, season=None)` — avg goals/match, home win rate, etc.\n11. `biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, limit=10)`.\n12. `team_search(query)` — list known teams matching a query (helps LLM resolve names).\n13. `list_competitions()` — list available competitions.\n\nThat's a good set covering the 5 query categories.\n\nThe spec also mentions a \"knowledge graph interface\" — but no graph DB required (the optional Neo4j was only in the sample prompt in prompts.txt, which is ignored; TASK.md is the spec, and it doesn't mandate Neo4j). I'll implement an in-memory normalized model. I could frame it as a lightweight in-memory graph (entities + relations) but that's overkill. The required capabilities are what matter.\n\nAlso BDD tests using GWT structure (Given/When/Then) as in TASK.md's \"Testing Approach\". I'll write pytest tests with docstrings containing Gherkin and test names like `test_...`, plus maybe a small GWT helper. Keep it plain pytest with clear Given/When/Then comments/docstrings — that satisfies \"BDD GWT structured PyTest\".\n\nStructure:\n```\n/workspace/\n├── soccer_mcp/\n│   ├── __init__.py\n│   ├── models.py       (dataclasses)\n│   ├── normalize.py    (team name normalization, date parsing)\n│   ├── data_loader.py  (load CSVs -> SoccerData)\n│   ├── queries.py      (query functions)\n│   └── server.py       (MCP server with tools)\n├── server.py           (entry point: python server.py)\n├── requirements.txt\n├── pytest.ini / pyproject.toml\n├── tests/\n│   ├── conftest.py\n│   ├── test_match_queries.py\n│   ├── test_team_queries.py\n│   ├── test_player_queries.py\n│   ├── test_competition_queries.py\n│   └── test_statistics.py\n└── README.md\n```\n\nEach code file needs a detailed context block comment at the start (from the original prompt style in prompts.txt — \"Maintain a detailed context block comment at the start of every code file\"). That was for the hive-mind prompt, but TASK.md is the governing spec. The user's instruction: \"Read TASK.md and implement everything it asks for.\" TASK.md doesn't require the context block. But it doesn't hurt and matches the repo's original intent. I'll include module docstrings with context.\n\nNow check overlaps. Let me look at seasons per file and check Brasileirao_Matches vs novo_campeonato_brasileiro overlap.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0634da547001mT0JKcTBCR6D34","sessionID":"ses_f9cb500feffeJAzwf2KlQEhiz0","messageID":"msg_0634d3d420015sPR32hJyJCmK3"}}
