\n'Sao Raimundo AM' 1\n'Sao Raimundo PA' 1\n'Sao Raimundo RR' 3\n'Sport Recife' 397\n'Vasco Da Gama RJ' 419\n'Vitoria' 371\n'Vitoria EC' 11\n'Vitoria ES' 3\n'Vitoria da Conquista' 6\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd /tmp/opencode && python3 << 'EOF'\nimport csv\nfrom collections import Counter\nnames = Counter()\nwith open('/workspace/data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    for r in csv.DictReader(f):\n        names[r['home']] += 1; names[r['away']] += 1\nfor n in sorted(names):\n    import re\n    if re.search(r'[Aa]tl|thl|Parana|Mineiro|Goiani|Alago|São|Sao|Paulo|Sport|Recife|Cear|Vasco|Goi|Forte|Av|Vitor|Vit', n):\n        print(repr(n), names[n])\nEOF","time":{"start":1788372481200,"end":1788372481208}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The (season, home, away) key isn't unique within a single file either (2012 had a non-standard format with some pairs playing 3 times? The conflicts show ('BM', ...) twice with different scores — so within Brasileirao_Matches 2012, e.g. pontepreta vs atletico appears twice as home for pontepreta). 2012 Serie A had 20 teams... Actually the \"atletico\" base name is ambiguous! \"atletico\" could be Atlético-MG OR Atlético-PR in Brasileirao_Matches, and Athletico-PR also normalizes to \"athletico\"... wait no, in 2012 Serie A both Atlético-MG and Atlético-PR participated! Both normalize to \"atletico\" when state suffix is stripped. That's why there are \"duplicates\" — they're actually different matches (Atlético-MG vs Ponte Preta AND Atlético-PR vs Ponte Preta).\n\nSo dedup by (season, home_base, away_base) is invalid when base names collide. I must keep the state in the key: e.g. \"atleticomg\" vs \"atleticopr\".\n\nThis confirms the canonical key design: **base + state**. For \"Atlético-MG\" key = \"atleticomg\"; \"Atlético-PR\" = \"atleticopr\". For names without state (BR-FD \"Sao Paulo\"), we need to resolve to the state'd canonical via a mapping (e.g., most prominent club with that base).\n\nDesign the resolution pipeline:\n1. Raw name → parse: strip parenthetical (capture country if present), extract trailing state suffix if present.\n2. base_key (accent-stripped alphanumeric lowercase), state (upper or None), country (upper or None).\n3. Canonical identity = base_key + \":\" + (state or country or \"\")... but state-less names must resolve. Build from data: for every distinct (base_key, state) observed, count matches. For a stateless name, resolve to the (base_key, state) with the most matches in the dataset (famous club wins: \"Flamengo\" → RJ not PI). But \"atletico\" stateless in BR-FD → both atleticomg and atleticopr have many matches... BR-FD Serie A rows with \"Atletico\" — which one is it? BR-FD names are from a different source; \"Atletico Mineiro\" appears in FIFA. Let me check BR-FD distinct names containing \"tletico\".\n\nHmm. Let me check how BR-FD spells the two Atléticos.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0634e6bb1001g8o5YBipucTNKV","sessionID":"ses_f9cb500feffeJAzwf2KlQEhiz0","messageID":"msg_0634e37b1001pRcHAe9dLd2N6g"}}
